declare const tools: { mcp__pwsh__execute_command(args: {
  // Agent ID for sub-agent console isolation. Obtain this by calling start_console with is_subagent=true. Do not pass arbitrary strings.
  agent_id?: string | null;
  // Set to true if you are a sub-agent. A unique agent_id will be allocated and returned in the response. Use that agent_id for all subsequent tool calls.
  is_subagent?: boolean;
  // The PowerShell command or pipeline to execute. Multi-line commands (if, loops, try-catch, etc.) are supported.
  pipeline: string;
  // Timeout in seconds (default: 170). Automatically capped to what the connected MCP client is known to wait for, so a longer value is silently reduced. On timeout, execution continues in background and result is cached for retrieval on next tool call. PowerShell host prompts (Read-Host, Get-Credential, a missing mandatory parameter) return control immediately as awaiting_input regardless of this value. Use 0 for native CLIs that wait on stdin (e.g., cmd /c pause, ssh, npm login) so the call returns at once instead of waiting out the timeout.
  timeout_seconds?: number;
  // Literal string value injected as $var1 in the pipeline, bypassing the PowerShell parser.
  var1?: string | null;
  // Literal string value injected as $var2 in the pipeline, bypassing the PowerShell parser.
  var2?: string | null;
  // Literal string value injected as $var3 in the pipeline, bypassing the PowerShell parser.
  var3?: string | null;
  // Literal string value injected as $var4 in the pipeline, bypassing the PowerShell parser.
  var4?: string | null;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```"},{"name":"mcp__pwsh__start_console","description":"Ensure a PowerShell console is available, or launch a new one. When reason is empty or omitted, reuses an existing standby console if one is available. When reason is provided, always launches a new console regardless of existing ones.

📌 Calling this first is optional — execute_command auto-starts or reuses a console as needed. Call it explicitly to pre-warm a console at a specific start_location, to show a startup banner, or (with reason) to force an additional console.

